  Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_00(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_01(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_02(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_03(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_04(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_05(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_06(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_07(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_08(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_09(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_10(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_11(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_12(), tail: Cons { head: floor_cost_debt_chunk_13(), tail: Empty {} } } } } } } } } } } } } } }
}

fn floor_cost_debt_roster() -> List<String> {
  list_flat_map(xs: floor_cost_debt_chunks(), f: fn(chunk) { chunk })
}

// ── THE DECLARED RUNG DROP (DESIGN §4b(3)) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// WHAT IS LOST, AT THE HONEST GRAIN: 266 witnesses stop executing. Not "run less often", not
// "run elsewhere" -- they run NOWHERE, and every property each one asserts is unguarded from the
// moment this lands. It is ~2.1% of the fold's 12936 identities.
//
// PREVIOUS RUNG: mechanically preventable -- each of these executed on every required run, so a
// regression in the property it asserts would have failed the floor.
// TEMPORARY RUNG: outside the ladder. A withheld row is not MITIGATED, because nothing is
// attempted; no weaker check stands behind it and review diligence cannot read it.
//
// BOUNDED POPULATION: the 266 identities enumerated above, measured on main runs 33141550579 and 33145062452 and on no
// other. Bounded in the strong sense §4b(3) asks for -- the universe is discovered by the floor's
// own terminal ledger rather than authored, membership is at identity grain, and the roster is
// SHRINK-ONLY: the two-way stale join refuses the run when an enrolled identity no longer
// corresponds to a discovered site, so a row cannot be quietly retained after its subject moves,
// and no row can be added without a measurement that names it.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: a row leaves when its MARGINAL
// CPU, measured by the required floor on a tree carrying the fill attribution, falls at or under
// `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` -- at which point it is deleted from the roster and
// simply executes. The capability being restored is the EXECUTION of these assertions, so nothing
// short of a row running and passing under the ceiling retires it. In particular: a plan to make
// it cheap does not, a measurement on a tree without the fill bracket does not, and neither does
// RAISING THE CEILING, which would retire the row by moving the line rather than restoring
// anything.
//
// WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS DROP DISHONEST: reporting the roster as a cost-management success. It is
// not. It is 266 assertions that no longer run, carried because the alternative was a floor that
// cannot green, and the correct end state is an EMPTY roster reached by making the work cheap --
// never this file stabilising at a comfortable size.

// THE MEMBERSHIP TEST. A `Bool` over a roster is the right shape here and not the mechanical
// one DESIGN warns about: the question this answers has exactly two states -- withheld or not --
// and the typed disposition that carries WHY lives in `v2.workflow.required_floor`, which is
// where a consumer that needs the reason reads it.
fn floor_cost_debt_holds(name: String) -> Bool {
  any(xs: floor_cost_debt_roster(), predicate: fn(row) { row == name })
}

